WEBVTT

00:00:00.184 --> 00:00:03.530
Remote access allows vendors, integrators, engineers,

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:06.570
and support teams to maintain geographically distributed systems.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.930
It can improve response time and reduce travel,

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.400
but it also creates a pathway across physical and network boundaries.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.610
The security objective is to preserve legitimate support while making access controlled,

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.880
attributable, limited, and observable.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.630
Virtual private networks provide encrypted connectivity,

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.510
but a VPN alone does not answer who should connect, from what device, to which system,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.468
for how long, or with what privileges.

00:00:27.468 --> 00:00:30.080
Route access through managed gateways or jump hosts.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.180
Use individual accounts, strong authentication, explicit approval, least privilege,

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.355
and time-limited sessions.

00:00:35.355 --> 00:00:39.045
Shared vendor credentials weaken accountability and make offboarding difficult.

00:00:39.045 --> 00:00:43.060
Permanently enabled accounts and tunnels expand exposure beyond the maintenance task.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.860
Unmanaged third-party laptops may carry vulnerabilities or software outside the

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.160
organization's control.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.610
Define endpoint requirements and use controlled transfer methods for files and updates.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:56.395
Monitoring should capture authentication, session start and end, destinations,

00:00:56.395 --> 00:00:59.075
administrative activity, and unusual use.

00:00:59.075 --> 00:01:02.110
Recording an interactive session may be appropriate for sensitive work,

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.340
subject to policy and operational constraints.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.600
Logs need review; collecting them without ownership does not reduce risk.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.200
Third-party governance begins before access is granted.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.840
Contracts, contacts, incident notification, security responsibilities,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.335
and emergency revocation should be clear.

00:01:17.335 --> 00:01:18.680
Inventory every remote pathway,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.770
including vendor appliances and cellular links that may bypass central controls.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:23.730
During an incident,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:27.610
the organization should be able to identify active sessions and disable access without

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:28.325
improvisation.

00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:31.050
Secure remote access is a managed operational service,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.280
not a collection of forgotten connections.
